<section #sec:tables | Tables>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A table is `<table>` with a format and the data written inline. The first row is the header by default, and a `caption` titles it:

<code-block #code:table-basic>
<table csv caption="Quarterly revenue">
Quarter, Revenue, Growth
Q1, 1.2M, —
Q2, 1.5M, 25%
Q3, 1.9M, 27%
</table>
</code-block>

<minipage #mp:table-basic>
<table csv caption="Quarterly revenue">
Quarter, Revenue, Growth
Q1, 1.2M, —
Q2, 1.5M, 25%
Q3, 1.9M, 27%
</table>
</minipage>

The same data can be written as `tsv`, `json`, `yaml`, or `md` — the format names the shape of the inline content.

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
Attribute, Example
format (the shape of the inline data), csv | tsv | json | yaml | md
caption (the table's title), caption="Quarterly revenue"
type (a semantic class), data | layout | comparison | schedule | results | other
No header row, -headers
Unnumbered (drop the "Table N." label), -numbered
parse-columns (named columns parse as Enscribe markup), parse-columns=Notes
src (load the data from an external file), src=data/revenue.csv
Id, #tab:revenue
Class, .striped
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Tables are numbered by default and captioned "Table N." — add `-numbered` for a plain reference table.

In the data formats (csv, tsv, json, yaml) cells are literal by default; `+parse-text` parses every cell as Enscribe markup, or name specific columns with `parse-columns`. Markdown (`md`) tables always parse.
</section>
